“AN ORDINANCE AMENDING TITLE 17, ZONING, CHAPTER 17.08, ZONING
DISTRICTS ESTABLISHED AND SECTION 17.08.040 CHEYENNE CITY MAP OF THE
CHEYENNE CITY CODE 2002, BY CHANGING THE ZONING CLASSIFICATION FROM
LR-2 LOW-DENSITY RESIDENTIAL-DEVELOPING DISTRICT TO NB NEIGHBORHOOD
BUSINESS DISTRICT FOR THE SOUTH 130-FEET OF THE WEST 100-FEET OF TRACT
2, SKYLINE TRACTS, CHEYENNE, WYOMING (LOCATED AT THE NORTHEAST
CORNER OF THE INTERSECTION OF STOREY BOULEVARD AND SYCAMORE
ROAD).”  Dr. Rinne declared a conflict of interest (nearby property owner interests) and vacated
Council Chambers.  Katharine Kunz, speaking in opposition to the zone change, submitted and
discussed a petition (on file with the City Clerk’s office) signed by 45 individuals, the majority
of whom reside within the general neighborhood area, who are opposed to the zone change, and
read the introduction of the petition.  Also speaking in opposition to the zone change were June
Lopez Wood, Terri Foley, Jack Knudson, Gloria Milmont, April Brimmer Kunz and Joe Kasper.
Topics of concern presented included: Zone change resulting in deterioration and disruption of
the neighborhood’s residential integrity; commercial development on north side of Storey Blvd.;
traffic and safety; PlanCheyenne guidelines for area growth; inconsistency of zone changes
involving commercial uses in established residential neighborhoods; changing the residential
character of the neighborhood; support for a Mixed Use Residential (MUR) zone instead of a
Neighborhood Business zone; past actions on the proposed ordinance by the governing body and
Planning Commission; provisions of Wyo. Stat. §15-1-601(d)(iv) and 15-1-603(a); petition that
had been submitted; governing body discretion with reference to potential arbitrary and
capricious actions; previous amendment to the ordinance involving conditional zoning and effect
on potential future property owner(s); previous actions by the governing body involving other
similar zone change requests, and the effect of “block busting” and precedence setting if the
zone change was approved.  Brandon Bailey spoke in favor of the zone change, expressing his
support for small businesses and the opportunity the business would provide local artists to
display and sell items.  Debb Roden, Woodhouse Law Office, attorney representing the property
owners, spoke in favor of the zone change and responded to the concerns that had been presented
involving traffic, PlanCheyenne, language contained in state statutes relating to zoning, and the
previously approved amendment concerning a conditional zoning provision that would limit the
types of uses allowed on the property.  Ms. Roden provided information on proposed business
hours and the types of items that would be available for sale; property owners’ compromise (i.e.
conditional zoning) efforts, and referenced a previous communication she had submitted
involving filing of the conditional zoning information with the County real estate office to assist
with title searches applicable to any future property owners of the site.  Ron Pinther commented
on a recent zone change request he had submitted that was denied by the governing body; spoke
in favor of implementing a neighborhood mediation process for zone change requests of this
type, and stated his support generally for this proposed zone change. Reporting for Public
Services Committee, Mr. Collins advised there was no recommendation on the item from the
Committee. Mr. Brown moved to approve on third and final reading, seconded by Mr. Segrave.
Comment followed on examples of other small businesses located within residential
environments, state statute phrase of “arbitrary and capricious” relative to the conditional zoning
amendment, and whether a possible MUR zone had been reviewed by staff.  Upon inquiry, Bob
Nicholas, Acting City Attorney, stated the phrase “arbitrary and capricious” is a legal term and
```

### Page 3 of 8

```text
any challenge to Council members’ actions would be resolved through a court of law, and
advised that the petition submitted appeared to not invoke a “super majority” vote requirement
(per state statute) as it had not been represented, only inferred, to Council that the signatures
received constituted 20% of the adjacent property owners located within 140 feet of the property
involved.  Martin Matsen, Development Director, confirmed a MUR zone district had been
discussed during the process and that a larger property coverage area was required, and the
applicants did not pursue a request for that zone type.  Discussion followed and included the
conditional zoning amendment previously approved, which limits the property’s commercial use
and affords some protection to the neighborhood; spot zoning; protection of established
residential neighborhoods; future uses of properties north of Storey; commercial business sites
available in the community, and concern with conditional zoning limitations generally.  Motion
carried. Voting “yes” – Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Mr. Pierson, Mr. Segrave, Mr. Valdez and
Mayor Spiker.  Voting “no” – Ms. Case, Mr. Collins and Mr. Laybourn voting “no”. Following
announcement of the vote, Dr. Rinne returned to Council Chambers.  (#3832)
