RIGHT-OF-WAY AND EAST OF AND INCLUDING I-25 RIGHT-OF-WAY).” Gay
Woodhouse, Woodhouse Law Office, attorney representing several property owners in the area,
referenced a letter she had previously submitted to the governing body on the proposed
annexation and stated her belief that compliance with annexation contiguity criteria had not been
met.  She distributed and reviewed information in support of her statements concerning
contiguity and explained the process utilized in measuring the distances and ascertaining the
percentage of shared common boundaries with the City (6.2%) which she feels does not meet
contiguity requirements.  Ms. Woodhouse displayed and discussed a 2004 City map, submitting
a handout depicting a portion of the map involving the property area, reflecting City boundaries
and property contiguity, and inside vs. outside growth of the City.  (Information submitted on
file with City Clerk’s office.)  She expressed concerns with public safety, points of access/egress
and barriers (right-of-way, railroad), traffic flow and volume of semi-truck traffic in the area,
and health, safety and welfare issues affecting the neighborhood. Speaking in opposition to the
proposed annexation were:  John Fafoutakis, David Wilkinson, Brenda Rath, Dean Marrs, Glenn
```

### Page 5 of 8

```text
Connor, Eleanor Sterman, Leo Garcia and Kay Sheehan.  Topics presented and discussed
included:  Area ingress/egress; timeliness of emergency responders; train crossing volume and
safety; traffic congestion; road surface conditions; Wyo. Dept. of Transportation and Laramie
County traffic count data (handout submitted and on file with City Clerk’s office); residential
composition of the neighborhood; hazardous materials transported by trains in the area;
suggestion the agenda item be postponed to acquire updated traffic count data; unknown
development/use of the property if annexed, and current vs. anticipated future traffic volume in
the area.  Brad Emmons, AVI Engineering, agent for the applicant, advised a definite use for the
property has not yet been disclosed by the owner but that any use must meet City development
and platting requirements.  Upon inquiry concerning the discrepancy in the percentage of
contiguity arrived upon by City staff vs. Ms. Woodhouse’s statements, Mr. Emmons advised the
surveyor and City staff should be the individuals to review the issue, and Ms. Woodhouse stated
her belief that staff’s computation included an additional 550 ft. which reflects a “point to point”
scenario and should not be included. Mr. Collins moved to adopt, seconded by Ms. Broyles.
Upon inquiry, Martin Matsen, Development Director, and Matt Ashby, Urban Planning Director,
provided information on the Unified Development Code (UDC) project and clarified during the
process, involving the consultant and public input meetings, a conceptual plan involving the area
was developed as an example to show how a new option for mixed uses under the proposed
UDC could be developed.  Upon inquiry, Brandon Cammarata, Urban Planning Office, reviewed
the process utilized in calculating the percentage of contiguity. Discussion included concern with
unknown future use of the property, developments in the area, quality and smart growth, and
property contiguity.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body with
the exception of Ms. Case, Mr. Laybourn and Mr. Valdez voting “no”.  (#5085)
